   "text": "Skip to content\nFriday, October 30, 2026\nthe5krunner\n\nEndurance & performance tech: news & opinion\n\nHome\nShop\nDeals\nInsider Info\nReviews\nPlans\nForum\nⓘ\nSupporter\nBest Fitness Tracker 2026 – Fitbit Edge Has No Real Rival\n\nHome » Best: Watches, Wearables and Cycling » Best wearables » Best Fitness Tracker 2026 – Fitbit Edge Has No Real Rival\n\nLast updated: 12 October 2026. This guide is updated when a new tracker launches or a price changes.\n\nThe best fitness tracker for most people is the Google Fitbit Edge at $179 (£159.99). It lets you run or ride without your phone, see your pace and heart rate at a glance, count the hills you climb and check your heart rhythm, and it lasts a week between charges. Very few trackers do all of that, and the table below shows why the Edge has so few real rivals.\n\nVideo Muted\nGoogle Fitbit Edge\n\nFitness tracker with a screen and GPS\n\n$179/£159.99/€179.99\n\nGet it now\n– Google Fitbit Edge\nEvery fitness tracker at a glance\nModel\tUS price\tBuilt-in GPS\tScreen\tPayments\tFeatures\tBest on\nSamsung Galaxy Fit3\tabout $50\tNo\tYes\tNo\tFew\tAndroid\nAmazfit Bip 6\t$71\tYes\tYes\tNo\tSome\tiPhone, Android\nXiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro\tabout $80\tYes\tYes\tNFC edition only\tFew\tiPhone, Android\nHuawei Band 11 Pro\t£69.99 (not sold in the US)\tYes\tYes\tNo\tFew\tHuawei, Android, iPhone\nAmazfit Bip Max\t$90\tYes\tYes\tNo\tSome\tiPhone, Android\nFitbit Inspire 3\tabout $99\tNo\tYes\tNo\tFew\tiPhone, Android\nFitbit Air\t$99.99\tNo\tNo\tNo\tSome\tiPhone, Android\nFitbit Charge 6\tabout $130\tYes\tYes\tYes\tSome\tiPhone, Android\nGarmin Vivosmart 5\t$149.99\tNo\tYes\tNo\tFew\tiPhone, Android\nFitbit Edge\t$179\tYes\tYes\tNo\tLots\tiPhone, Android\nHuawei Watch Fit 5\t£159.99 (not sold in the US)\tYes\tYes\tYes\tLots\tHuawei, Android, iPhone\nAmazfit Active 3 Premium\t$169.99\tYes\tYes\tYes\tSome\tiPhone, Android\nApple Watch SE 3\tfrom $249\tYes\tYes\tYes\tLots\tiPhone\nGarmin Forerunner 70\t$249.99\tYes\tYes\tNo\tLots\tiPhone, Android\n\nRows are shaded by price bracket: under $100, $100 to $150, $150 to $200, and $200 and up. “Features” counts six things a tracker can let you do: track hills and floors, check your heart rhythm, coach you or give you a training plan, show offline maps, take calls or reply to messages, and replace a worn battery. Four or more scores “Lots”, two or three “Some”, and one or none “Few”. Prices are typical US street prices in October 2026.\n\nQuick picks\nBest overall: Fitbit Edge. No other band sold in the US lets you train without your phone, check your heart rhythm and count your hills, on iPhone or Android.\nBest on a Huawei or Android phone: Huawei Watch Fit 5. It matches the Edge and adds wrist payments and calls.\nCheapest way to leave your phone at home: Xiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro.\nBest big screen for the money: Amazfit Bip 6.\nSimplest Fitbit: Fitbit Inspire 3, for sleep, steps and heart rate without the extras.\nBest smartwatch for iPhone: Apple Watch SE 3, if you will charge it every night.\nBest for running: Garmin Forerunner 70.\nUnder $100: GPS is what separates them\n\nTrackers in this bracket without built-in GPS are not real competitors to the Edge. The Samsung Galaxy Fit3 and Fitbit Inspire 3 borrow your phone’s GPS, so you have to carry it to record a run or ride. The others let you leave the phone at home and last far longer between charges than the Edge, but they cannot check your heart rhythm and their coaching is basic.\n\nXiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro\n\nThe Smart Band 10 Pro is the cheapest way to record a route without your phone, in a band about the size of the Edge. Battery lasts up to three weeks with light use. There is no hill tracking, heart-rhythm check or coaching, and wrist payments need the separate NFC edition. In the US, Amazon only sells it through third-party resellers.\n\nXiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro\n\nA slim band that tracks runs and rides with its own GPS and goes up to three weeks between charges.\n\n£69.99\n\n€69.99\n\nGet it now\n– Xiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro\nHuawei Band 11 Pro\n\nThe Band 11 Pro is the closest band to the Edge for under £70. It records routes without your phone, flags possible atrial fibrillation in some countries and lasts 8 to 14 days. Its GPS is only good for rough tracking, and there are no payments, calls or hill tracking. Huawei does not sell it in the US.\n\nHuawei Band 11 Pro\n\nRecord runs and rides without your phone, with up to two weeks between charges, in a slim alloy band.\n\n£55.59\n\n€54.99\n\nGet it now\n– Huawei Band 11 Pro\nAmazfit Bip 6\n\nThe Bip 6 offers a big, bright screen, built-in GPS, free offline maps, and calls through your phone for about $71. Battery lasts up to two weeks. The Bip 6 is a slim watch rather than a band, with no payments or heart-rhythm checks.\n\nAmazfit Bip 6\n\nA big-screen GPS watch that maps your runs without a phone and lasts up to two weeks.\n\n$70.99\n\n€70.96\n\nGet it now\n– Amazfit Bip 6\nAmazfit Bip Max\n\nThe Bip Max is a bigger Bip with offline maps and up to 20 days of battery for $90. It does more than any band at this price, but the Zepp app doesn’t integrate with your phone as closely as Google’s or Apple’s, and the large case makes it a watch, not a band.\n\nAmazfit Bip Max\n\nA big-screen GPS watch with offline maps and up to 20 days of battery, for less than most bands.\n\n$89.99\n\n€84.49\n\nGet it now\n– Amazfit Bip Max\nFitbit Inspire 3\n\nThe Inspire 3 is the simplest Fitbit with a screen. It tracks sleep, steps and heart rate for up to 10 days and uses your phone’s GPS for workouts. As a first tracker it works well, but it cannot record a route on its own, pay or check your heart rhythm. Buyers often weigh it against the Charge 6, which adds GPS and payments.\n\nFitbit Inspire 3\n\nFitbit's simplest tracker with a screen, for sleep, heart rate and workouts, using your phone's GPS and lasting up to 10 days.\n\n€48.04\n\nGet it now\n– Fitbit Inspire 3\nSamsung Galaxy Fit3\n\nThe Galaxy Fit3 tracks steps, sleep and workouts for about two weeks, for about $50. You need your phone for GPS, and it works only with Android phones.\n\nSamsung Galaxy Fit3\n\nA low-cost band for steps, sleep and workouts. Android only.\n\n$59\n\n€69\n\nGet it now\n– Samsung Galaxy Fit3\n$100 to $150: a screen is what separates them\n\nTrackers in this bracket without a screen aren’t real competitors to the Edge because they cannot show you anything during a workout. The Fitbit Air is the clearest example.\n\nFitbit Air\n\nThe Air is a screenless band for sleep, recovery and daily activity, with the same heart rate hardware and app as the Edge. At $99.99 it is cheaper, but you check everything in the app afterwards, and it needs your phone to record a route. Read the Fitbit Air review for the accuracy tests. The Garmin CIRQA is the other screenless band worth knowing about, at $199.99.\n\nGoogle Fitbit Air\n\nActivity tracker\n\n$99.99/£84.99/€99.00\n\nGet it now\n– Google Fitbit Air\nFitbit Charge 6\n\nThe Charge 6 is the Edge’s predecessor and is now discounted to about $130. Unlike the Edge, it lets you pay from your wrist, and it checks your heart rhythm too. Its GPS was weak, its screen is small, and it cannot count hills. See the Fitbit tracker upgrade guide if you already own one.\n\nFitbit Charge 6\n\nFitness tracker with SpO2 and skin temperature sensing, Cardio Fitness Level, Sleep Score and up to 7 days of battery life. Heart rate tracking is rated up to 60% more accurate than other Fitbit trackers during vigorous activity.\n\n$159.95\n\n£139.99\n\nGet it now\n– Fitbit Charge 6\nGarmin Vivosmart 5\n\nThe Vivosmart 5 brings Garmin’s Body Battery energy score and sleep tracking to a slim band. There is no GPS of its own, no payments and only a small screen, and Garmin no longer sells any band with built-in GPS.\n\nGarmin Vivosmart 5\n\nSimple, easy-to-use fitness tracker with Pulse Ox, Body Battery and up to 7 days of battery life.\n\nfrom $149.99 / £129.99\n\nGet it now\n– Garmin Vivosmart 5\n$150 to $200: the real contest\n\nThis is the only bracket where the Edge has a true rival, and that rival is built for a different phone.\n\nFitbit Edge\n\nThe Edge lets you record walks, runs and rides without your phone, and Google claims a new GPS chip and an improved antenna design make it 40% more accurate than the Charge 6. GPS is dynamic: connected (using your phone’s GPS) or standalone. The 46% larger screen makes pace and heart rate easy to read mid-workout, and an altimeter counts the hills and stairs you climb. ECG checks your heart rhythm, Google Health Premium adds a coach, and up to 100 workouts are recognised automatically, so a forgotten start button no longer means a lost session. Battery life is up to seven days, and you can replace the battery yourself. The one thing missing is tap-and-pay. Full details are on the Fitbit Edge specifications page.\n\nGoogle Fitbit Edge\n\nFitness tracker with a screen and GPS\n\n$179/£159.99/€179.99\n\nGet it now\n– Google Fitbit Edge\nHuawei Watch Fit 5\n\nThe Watch Fit 5 matches the Edge on paper and goes further in places. It records routes without your phone, stores maps and music, checks your heart rhythm, takes calls and lets you pay from your wrist. Huawei and Android phones get the most from it. It also works with iPhone, but few iPhone owners choose it, and Huawei does not sell it in the US. See the Watch Fit 5 specifications.\n\nHuawei Watch Fit 5\n\nHeart rate, SpO2 and sleep tracking with a mini-workout mode, in a lightweight aluminium case rated for up to 10 days of battery life.\n\n£159.00\n\nGet it now\n– Huawei Watch Fit 5\nAmazfit Active 3 Premium\n\nThe Active 3 Premium has offline maps, payments, and about 12 days of battery life, but it is a round watch rather than a band. It suits buyers who want a sports watch, not a tracker, so it doesn’t compete with the Edge.\n\nAmazfit Active 3 Premium\n\nGPS running smartwatch\n\n$169.99\n\n£169 / €169\n\nGet it now\n– Amazfit Active 3 Premium\n$200 and up: better, and dearer\n\nThese aren’t real competitors to the Edge because they are clearly more capable and cost more.\n\nApple Watch SE 3\n\nThe SE 3 lets you pay with Apple Pay, take calls, use apps and track workouts with built-in GPS. You have to charge it every night, and it works only with an iPhone. As a smartwatch it beats the Edge, but anyone who wants to track sleep without a charging routine is better off with the Edge. See the Apple Watch SE 3 specifications.\n\nApple Watch SE 3\n\nSmart fitness watch\n\nfrom $249\n\n£219, €269\n\nGet it now\n– Apple Watch SE 3\nGarmin Forerunner 70\n\nThe Forerunner 70 gives runners Garmin’s training plans, a daily readiness score, incident detection with live location sharing, and up to 13 days of battery life. This is a proper running watch with a bigger case, and it costs $70 more than the Edge. Read the Forerunner 70 review.\n\nGarmin Forerunner 70\n\nGPS running watch\n\n$249.99\n\n£219.99\n\nGet it now\n– Garmin Forerunner 70\nWhy the Edge has no direct rival\n\nI think the gap exists because few companies can make money from a $179 tracker. Google can because Fitbit sells in huge volumes, feeds Google’s health platform, trains its AI models, generates subscriptions, and integrates better with Android than any rival. Buyers also trust the Fitbit name, which Xiaomi and Huawei cannot yet match in the US, and Garmin, which made GPS bands years ago, no longer sells one.\n\nThe Edge also makes the Apple Watch SE look expensive. From $249 the SE does far more, but many buyers only want a tracker that lasts a week. That leaves room for something cheaper than the SE, and Apple is unlikely to fill it.\n\nFitness trackers for children\n\nThe Fitbit Ace LTE is Google’s watch for children. It lets parents see where their child is and lets the child call and message approved contacts, with games that reward movement. It requires a monthly Ace Pass data plan, is sold only in the US, and now often sells for around $100, well below its $229.95 launch price.\n\nThe Garmin Bounce 2 does the same job. Parents can see where their child is and set safe zones, and the child can call and message family over LTE. It costs $299.99 plus a $9.99 monthly plan, is also sold in the UK, and has a brighter screen than the Ace LTE. There is no heart rate sensor, so it counts steps and activity but not effort.\n\nGarmin Bounce 2\n\nConnected kids' smartwatch\n\n$299+subscription\n\n£259, €299\n\nGet it now\n– Garmin Bounce 2\nFitness trackers for smaller wrists\n\nThe Edge comes with a small band in the box for wrists from 130mm to 170mm. The Inspire 3 and Xiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro are the slimmest options here. The Forerunner 70 has a 43mm case that suits smaller wrists better than most running watches. The Garmin Lily 2 Active is a small, jewellery-style watch with built-in GPS, at $299.99.\n\nQuick answers\n\nWhat is the best fitness tracker in 2026?\nThe Fitbit Edge, for most people. It records routes without your phone, checks your heart rhythm, counts hills and lasts a week, for $179.\n\n\n\n\nFitness tracker or smartwatch?\nA fitness tracker is lighter, lasts a week or more and focuses on activity, sleep and health. A smartwatch adds apps, payments and calls, but usually needs charging every day or two. The Fitbit Edge sits between the two.\n\n\n\n\nShould I just buy a Fitbit?\nFor most people, yes. The Fitbit Edge works with iPhone and Android, has built-in GPS and heart-rhythm checks, and comes from a brand with a mature app. Choose a Huawei on a Huawei or Android phone, or an Apple Watch if you want payments and apps on an iPhone.\n\n\n\n\nWhat is the best Fitbit alternative?\nThe Huawei Watch Fit 5 is the closest match, best with Huawei and Android phones. The Xiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro is the cheapest alternative with built-in GPS.\n\n\n\n\nFitbit or Garmin?\nGarmin has no band with built-in GPS. Choose the Fitbit Edge for a tracker, or the Garmin Forerunner 70 if you want a running watch with training plans and can spend $70 more.\n\n\n\n\nFitbit or Apple Watch?\nThe Apple Watch SE 3 is the better smartwatch but needs charging every night and works only with iPhone. The Fitbit Edge lasts a week and works with iPhone and Android.\n\n\n\n\nAmazfit or Fitbit?\nAmazfit’s Bip 6 and Bip Max cost less and last longer. Fitbit’s Edge checks your heart rhythm, coaches you, and integrates more closely with your phone.\n\n\n\n\nWhich fitness trackers have built-in GPS?\nThe Fitbit Edge, Fitbit Charge 6, Xiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro, Huawei Band 11 Pro, Amazfit Bip 6, Amazfit Bip Max and Huawei Watch Fit 5. The Inspire 3, Galaxy Fit3, Vivosmart 5 and Fitbit Air use your phone’s GPS.\n\n\n\n\nWhat is the cheapest fitness tracker with GPS?\nThe Amazfit Bip 6 at about $71, followed by the Xiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro at about $80.\n\n\n\n\nWhat is the best Fitbit for smaller wrists?\nThe Fitbit Inspire 3 is the slimmest. The Fitbit Edge comes with a small band for 130mm to 170mm wrists.\n\nFurther reading\nFitbit Edge: all you need to know\nFitbit Edge vs Fitbit Air\nFitbit tracker upgrade guide\nBest recovery tracker\nFive screenless trackers compared\nGoogle Fitbit hub\nXiaomi Smart Band 10 Pro official specifications\nSmall things I always use\nGarmin 90-degree charging adapter: Charges your Garmin from any USB-C cable. Essential when travelling. I use one.\nBody Glide: The blue anti-chafe stick swimmers and runners carry. I use it.\nSpeedo earplugs: Fewer ear infections and no cold-water dizziness. I use them.\nDryrobe Advance: My winter changing robe for open-water swims. I use one. A cheaper option is the AONYIYI changing robe.\nMaurten: Race fuel that stays easy on the stomach. I use them.\nBeet It Sport nitrate shots: Beetroot shots I take in the days before a race. I use them.\nGarmin charging puck: The quickest way to top up before a session. I use one.\nInjinji: Toe socks that stop rubbing, so you run more. I use them.\nUltimate toe protectors: Stop black toenails on long runs. They work. I use them.\nCompressport R2 v2 calf sleeves: My calf sleeves of choice. I use them.\nCellucor C4: My pre-workout before hard sessions.\nTPU tubes: Schwalbe Aerothan, Tubolito and Pirelli SmarTube. Light, tiny spares. I use all three.\nG8 insoles: Adjustable insoles that are good. I use them.\nCycplus Cube: A 97g electric pump that gets a road tyre rideable in two minutes. I carry one. 5% off with code THE5KRUNNER.\nWatches and sensors\nPolar H10: My daily driver for waking HRV.\nPolar Verity Sense: My backup arm-worn HR sensor.\nNomad bedside charger: Charges my iPhone and Apple Watch overnight. I use it, and many of their bands.\nTrain.Red: The best muscle oxygen sensor I’ve used.\nTymewear: Accurate heart rate and reliable ventilatory zones. I use it to pace endurance rides over an hour.\nGarmin Forerunner 970: A pro-grade triathlon watch. I use this.\nApple Watch Ultra 4: My daily smartwatch. No phone needed on rides.\nGarmin Fenix 9 Pro: I’ve just bought one.\nOn the bike\nContinental GP5000 for everyday riding, and Aero 111 on the front for special occasions.\nHelmets: Specialized S-Works Evade 3 for road riding, and Rudy Project Wing for TT-style races. I use both.\nRavemen FR300: A front light that mounts under your Garmin or Wahoo. I use one.\nWahoo TRACKR Radar: I use it on one bike and Varia on the other. They perform the same.\nGarmin Varia RTL515: Radar that warns you of cars from behind. I use this model.\nWahoo ELEMNT Roam 3: The bike computer that’s easy to use. I use mine on most rides.\nFavero Assioma Pro RS2: Accurate power pedals that swap easily between bikes. I use this model.\nHome and indoor training\nNPE Runn: Makes my basic treadmill smart, sending more accurate speed and distance to my watch and Zwift. I use it.\nGoogle Wifi: Keeps Zwift connected in my pain cave. I use it.\nApple TV 4K: Zwift on the big screen. I use two.\nGoogle Nest Doorbell (wired, 2nd gen): My video doorbell, running in both Google Home and Apple Home. I use it.\nHealth, recovery and sleep\nFreeStyle Libre 3 Plus: Shows how my blood sugar responds to diet.\nhDrop: Measures my sweat loss in summer and indoors.\nFourth Frontier Zone: Checks my heart under load in hard sessions.\nOura Ring 5: My partner’s recovery tracker of choice.\nEight Sleep: A cooling mattress cover my partner and I use nightly.\nNewsletter\n\nSign up for the newsletter — no spam, just good stuff\n\nSubscribe\n\nYour email is safe with me — never sold, never shared. Unsubscribe anytime. Privacy Policy.\n\nReader-Powered Content\n\nThis content is not sponsored. It’s mostly me behind the labour of love, which is this site, and I appreciate everyone who supports it.\n\nSupport the site: Follow (free, fewer ads) · Subscribe (paid, ad-free) · Buy Me A Coffee\n\nWritten by real people, with AI assistance. Fact-checked and verified for originality. See the Editorial Policy. FTC: Affiliate Disclosure — some links pay commission. As an Amazon Associate, I earn from qualifying purchases.\n\nCopyright © 2026 the5krunnerPrivacy Policy\nTerms of Content Use\nA RAPTIVE PARTNER SITE",
