gs as the feature they use most.\n Sleep Tracking Is the Feature Owners Rate Highest\n Sleep tracking with a sleep score in the Fitbit app is the most consistently praised capability, appearing near the top of the review summary alongside the interchangeable bracelet. On a small wrist this matters more than on a large one, because the device is light enough to keep on overnight and the sleep data is the reason most people buy a tracker at all.\n You also get breathing rate and heart rate variability data, 24/7 heart rate monitoring, Active Zone Minutes with a buzz at target zones, and real-time pace and distance through the phone connection. That is a solid feature set for something that reads like a dress watch.\n The Hidden Cost Is Battery and the Premium Paywall\n Up to 5 days of battery is the weak point, and reviewers compare it unfavourably to silicone fitness bands, which is fair. A bracelet-style tracker with a small colour display is simply working harder per charge, and if you are the kind of person who charges nightly anyway this will not matter to you.\n The second limit is the Premium trial. Health metrics beyond the basics are locked behind a Fitbit Premium trial for new and returning members who activate within 60 days, so the full breadth of the breathing and variability data is not permanent. Read that as a wellness feature set, not a medical one.\n Who This Is For\n Pick the Luxe if your wrist is small and your wardrobe is not casual, or if you have already tried a sporty tracker and hated how it looked. The review base here is by far the largest in the roundup, which means you will find owners describing your exact wrist size and use case.\n Skip it if charging every few days bothers you, or if you want the deepest sleep analytics in the category. For that, the Fitbit Charge 6 further down does more.\n Check Latest Price on Amazon \n 4. Xiaomi Smart Band Active – The Most Versatile That Still Weighs Only 30g\n MOST VERSATILE\n 1.47 inch AMOLED at 