st. Owners report that the included silicone band can pinch or irritate, and that the large band has too few holes to get a genuinely snug fit. On a small wrist that is a real problem, and the fix is a different strap rather than a different device.\n The second is app reliability. Chronic sync failures with the Fitbit and Google Health app are the dominant complaint in the review set, and several buyers describe dropping to three stars or lower over it. The hardware is not the issue; the software connection is.\n Who This Is For\n Choose the Charge 6 if phone-free GPS matters and you tolerate replacing the included band. The sleep data and the seven-day battery are the strongest combination among the Fitbit models listed here, for anyone training seriously.\n If you are on a very small wrist, verify the band hole spacing before committing, because that is the one part of this device that may not suit you out of the box.\n Check Latest Price on Amazon \n 8. Garmin Lily 2 – The Only Pick With a Published 35mm Case\n SMALLEST PUBLISHED CASE\n 35 mm metal case with patterned lens\n Up to 5 days battery\n Phone-connected GPS\n Sleep score, Body Battery and stress tracking\n Check Price \n Pros\n 35 mm case looks like a traditional watch rather than a slab\n Patterned lens stays attractive with the screen off\n Light at 0.8 ounces and comfortable for sleep\n Heart rate and steps reported more accurate than a Galaxy Watch\n Cons\n Proprietary clip charger is fiddly and the watch cannot restart without it\n Heart rate has stopped working for some users until recharged\n Touch button frequently becomes unresponsive\n No built-in GPS and no HRV data in the app\n The Lily 2 is the only tracker here where the manufacturer actually publishes a case diameter, and that single number, 35mm, is the reason it appears on almost every small-wrist recommendation. A 35mm metal case with a patterned lens that hides the display until you tap it reads as a real watch rather than a rectangle of black glass.\n At 0.8 ounces it is light enough to sleep in, and the health stack is genuinely good. Sleep score, respiration tracking, Body Battery energy monitoring, all-day stress tracking, heart rate alerts, hydration and menstrual cycle tracking are all on board, along with smart notifications, LiveTrack and incident detection.\n The Case Size Is the Whole Argument\n Small-wrist owners describe the 35mm case as looking like a traditional watch, and that is the correct framing. Below 36mm a case sits flat and stops looking out of proportion; above 44mm it overhangs the wrist bone on most small frames. The Lily 2 sits just inside the first threshold.\n The phone-connected GPS handles outdoor walks and runs accurately through the paired smartphone, which is fine unless you specifically want to leave the phone behind. Battery is up to 5 days, and owners report three to five in typical use.\n The Charger Is the Weak Link\n The proprietary clip-style charger has to be positioned just right, and the watch cannot be restarted without it. This is the most-cited drawback in the review set, and it is a real inconvenience when the device dies mid-workout.\n Beyond that, the touch button frequently becomes unresponsive for some users, and heart rate tracking has stopped working for some owners until the device was charged. There is also no HRV data in the app, and sleep tracking relies on manually set sleep and wake times, which does not work for shift workers.\n Who This Is For\n Buy the Lily 2 if you want the most watch-like object on the list and you care about stress and recovery data more than sport metrics. The patterned lens solves the black-slab problem that makes other trackers look like medical devices.\n Pass if you need built-in GPS, HRV, or a charger that does not require precision alignment. Each of those is a genuine limitation, not a nitpick.\n Check Latest Price on Amazon \n 9. PAKRYS Smart Bracelet – Narrowest Strap in the Roundup at 10.5mm\n BEST FOR