   "text": "Best Fitness Tracker 2026: Fitbits, Bands & Hybrids – Tech Advisor \n When you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn't affect our editorial independence .\n by Chris Martin \n Managing Editor, Tech Advisor \n Best Fitness Trackers 2026: Great wearables for health and wellness\n Best Picks \n Oct 7, 2026 \n It’s an exciting time in the world of fitness trackers. Not only do we have plenty of amazing, budget-friendly picks from the likes of Xiaomi, Honor and Huawei, but we’re starting to see more brands capitalise on the screenless design initially popularised by Whoop. All of this means that you have more choice than ever when it comes to the best fitness trackers to buy.\n Still, with so many aspects to consider, such as the workouts covered, overall battery life between charges, and efficiency when it comes to heart rate tracking, it can be all too easy to get lost in a sea of specs and marketing jargon. Thankfully, this is where the hard work of our expert team comes into play, as we actually put these fitness trackers to the test, seeing how well they perform in the real world so that our recommendations are based on tangible results. All you have to do is pick the one that makes the most sense for your needs.\n At a glance\n Best Overall\n Huawei Band 11 Pro\n ↓ See why we picked it \n Check \n Check \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Premium design & build\n Strong quality AMOLED display\n Good array of features for the price\n Solid battery life\n Cons\n GPS performance has flaws\n Very similar to Band 11\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Alternative\n Xiaomi Smart Band 10\n ↓ See why we picked it \n Check \n Check \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Stylish design\n Large, clear display\n Smooth software\n 5ATM waterproofing\n Cons\n Mi Fit app feels a bit clunky in places\n Heart rate tracking not impeccable\n Ceramic edition pushes price up\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Screenless Fitness Tracker\n Google Fitbit Air\n ↓ See why we picked it \n View Deal \n View Deal \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Incredibly light & comfortable\n Excellent app interface\n Fast charging\n Health Coach is game-changing\n Cons\n Best features need subscription\n No GPS\n No NFC for contactless payments\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Watch-style Tracker\n Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro\n ↓ See why we picked it \n Check \n Check \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Nice new mini-workouts feature\n Great range of features for the price\n Now includes a larger and brighter display\n Cons\n Tedious to separately Consent to all health and fitness features\n Smartwatch support not equal across iOS and Android\n Huawei Health app is too busy\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Affordable Garmin\n Garmin Cirqa Smart Band\n ↓ See why we picked it \n Check \n Check \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Good value for money\n Comfortable, light design\n Dedicated workout button\n Plenty of subscription-free data\n Cons\n Relies heavily on the Garmin Connect app\n Missing some quality-of-life features\n Battery life shorter than stated\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Fitbit with a screen\n Fitbit Charge 6\n ↓ See why we picked it \n View Deal \n View Deal \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Google Maps and Google Pay access\n Great fitness features\n Impressive colour display\n Easy navigation\n Cons\n Some Google Health exclusives\n No altimeter\n Hit-and-miss GPS\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Screen\n Amazfit Bip 6\n ↓ See why we picked it \n View Deal \n View Deal \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Excellent screen\n Plenty of features\n Long battery life\n Very cheap\n Cons\n App is a bit busy\n An odd storage bug\n AI assistant a bit disjointed\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Hybrid\n Withings ScanWatch Light\n ↓ See why we picked it \n View Deal \n View Deal \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Stunning design\n Clever display\n Impressive battery life\n Lightweight yet durable\n Cons\n Hit-and-miss step and heart rate tracking\n Not good for notifications\n No built-in GPS\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Best Cheap Fitbit\n Fitbit Inspire 3\n ↓ See why we picked it \n View Deal \n View Deal \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Always-on colour screen\n Stylish & comfortable\n Comprehensive tracking\n Good battery life & charging\n Cons\n Tiny screen\n Google Health Premium needed for some features\n Short charging wire\n No GPS\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Most Stylish\n Garmin Lily 2\n ↓ See why we picked it \n View Deal \n View Deal \n Show Pros & Cons \n Pros\n Lightweight and elegant \n Range of features\n Decent battery life\n Body Battery and stress tracking are excellent\n Cons\n No GPS\n Sleep tracking could be better\n Women’s health needs improvement\n Hide Pros & Cons \n Show 5 More \n Show Top 5 only \n By design, fitness trackers tend to be a lot more affordable than the best smartwatches , with the trade-off being that you don’t have access to a wider app ecosystem. Still, if all you want is a means of staying on top of your health and recording your fitness journey, then you won’t miss those added features.\n However, if you are happy to save a little bit extra, you may be able to get those additional features for not that much more with one of the best budget smartwatches currently on the market. In all these cases, our team has given each device its due, so you’ll be getting an incredible wearable regardless of which one you buy.\n For something a bit more discreet, there’s also our guide to the best smart rings , ready for your perusal.\n Best fitness tracker reviews\n 1. Huawei Band 11 Pro – Best Overall \n Pros\n Premium design & build\n Strong quality AMOLED display\n Good array of features for the price\n Solid battery life\n Cons\n GPS performance has flaws\n Very similar to Band 11\n Price When Reviewed: \n Price TBC \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n Check \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n Although it might be tempting to opt for the slightly cheaper Huawei Band 11, we recommend that everyone chip in the extra £30 to get the Huawei Band 11 Pro as you’ll be getting a handful of tasty extras that are well worth the additional cost, the most notable of which is GPS connectivity.\n With GPS to hand, the Band 11 Pro can be used to track any outdoor runs, which is great for anyone who either doesn’t want to be confined to just the treadmill or would rather leave their phone at home whenever they put on their running shoes. Admittedly the performance isn’t as strong as the dual-band GPS you’ll find on more expensive Huawei watches, but for £69.99, it’s great to have it at all.\n Working in tandem with its GPS capabilities, the 11 Pro is also designed to better withstand the elements than its cheaper sibling. The aluminium frame is robust, and the wearable can still work whilst submerged in watery depth of up to 50 metres, so you won’t have anything to worry about if you accidentally spill your water bottle all over it.\n The 1.62-inch AMOLED display is very colourful, and you won’t have any issues with seeing what’s on screen when out in the sunshine. You can also get the Band 11 Pro to run for up to 14-days on a single charge, ideal for anyone who wants to wear the device 24/7 to track their rest and recovery.\n Who should buy the Huawei Band 11 Pro?\n Entry-level runners who want an affordable device with GPS tracking.\n Huawei Band 11 Pro: Further considerations\n If you don’t need GPS tracking, the Huawei Band 11 is cheaper.\n Read our full \n Huawei Band 11 Pro review \n 2. Xiaomi Smart Band 10 – Best Alternative \n Pros\n Stylish design\n Large, clear display\n Smooth software\n 5ATM waterproofing\n Cons\n Mi Fit app feels a bit clunky in places\n Heart rate tracking not impeccable\n Ceramic edition pushes price up\n Price When Reviewed: \n Unavailable in the US \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n Check \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n If you don’t have a lot of money to spend on a fitness tracker, but you’re also not a fan of having a bit of plastic strapped to your wrist, then Xiaomi’s Smart Band 10 is the budget option that feels far more premium than its price tag would suggest.\n Xiaomi ditched the plastic case a long time ago, now giving you the choice of aluminium or a new ceramic look. You can match that up with a TPU or flurorubber band or easily swap those out for a more metallic look or a knitted option. Don’t want to wear it on your wrist? There’s even a pendant accessory if you’d rather track from around your neck.\n That high-quality case now surrounds a larger 1.72-inch AMOLED display where the bezels have slimmed down around it, giving you more room for a great array of watch faces and readable notifications.\n The big fitness and health tracking changes lie with a new nine-axis sensor that notably improves the Band 10’s swim tracking abilities. Along with a surprisingly impressive heart rate tracking performance, you can now also broadcast HR to other devices, including cycling computers. There’s richer sleep tracking to delve into as well, helping you to make better decisions about your recovery time.\n While battery life remains the same 21 days (or nine days in always-on display mode) as the Band 9, it’s still a fitness tracker that will let you make the most of its strong monitoring abilities for well over a week.\n Who should buy the Xiaomi Smart Band 10?\n Anyone who wants a competent fitness tracker with all the key features at a reasonable price.\n Xiaomi Smart Band 10: Further considerations\n Some fitness trackers provide more accurate heart rate readings.\n Read our full \n Xiaomi Smart Band 10 review \n 3. Google Fitbit Air – Best Screenless Fitness Tracker \n Pros\n Incredibly light & comfortable\n Excellent app interface\n Fast charging\n Health Coach is game-changing\n Cons\n Best features need subscription\n No GPS\n No NFC for contactless payments\n Price When Reviewed: \n $99.99 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n $99.99 \n View Deal \n $99.99 \n View Deal \n $99.99 \n View Deal \n $99.99 \n View Deal \n $99.99 \n View Deal \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n View more prices \n For the longest time, the Whoop Strap dominated the screenless fitness tracker scene, building up a strong user base but also requiring a hefty annual subscription fee (though you get the device included). As much as the Whoop Strap will still have an audience amongst professional athletes who will make full use of the features available, for everyone else, the Fitbit Air has arrived to offer a proper, low-cost alternative.\n Without a screen, the Fitbit Air is a distraction-free device that can just crack on with measuring your heart rate, overall exertion throughout the day, signs of atrial fibrillation and, crucially, how well your body has rested during overnight sleep or during any daytime naps. This all combines to give you a better understanding of whether your body is hitting the right balance between exercise and recovery, something that a lot of fitness trackers have previously overlooked.\n It’s supremely comfortable to wear 24/7 and the battery lasts a week, which is decent. The pebble-like device can be easily removed to put into different bands and you can even use it alongside a Pixel Watch for more accurate data or chop and change between the two depending on what you’re doing.\n The Air connects to the vastly improved Google Health app (it replaces the Fitbit app), which is brilliantly designed, showing you all the key information you want to see at a glance, but what takes it to a whole other level is the AI-powered Health Coach. This Gemini-powered chatbot works like a personal trainer, giving you advice on what workouts to tackle and how to find a schedule that works to suit your needs. It feels like a whole new style of fitness tracking that actually responds to your needs and doesn’t lecture you.\n The only problem is that the Health Coach isn’t included with the Air by default; you need an active Google Health Premium subscription, which costs £7.99/$9.99 a month (or an annual fee). Thankfully, you do get three months of the service included for free with the Air, and with a library of workouts, meditations and recipes also available, we think it’s a worthy purchase for those who want to start their fitness journey off on the right foot.\n Who should buy the Fitbit Air?\n Folks who want a distraction-free fitness tracker that focuses on rest and recovery.\n Fitbit Air: Further considerations\n You’ll need to have a separate Google Health Premium subscription to make the most of the Fitbit Air.\n Read our full \n Google Fitbit Air review \n 4. Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro – Best Watch-style Tracker \n Pros\n Nice new mini-workouts feature\n Great range of features for the price\n Now includes a larger and brighter display\n Cons\n Tedious to separately Consent to all health and fitness features\n Smartwatch support not equal across iOS and Android\n Huawei Health app is too busy\n Price When Reviewed: \n Not available in the US \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n Check \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n Huawei’s Watch Fit lineup has long offered some of the best fitness trackers we’ve ever tested and that trend only continues with the Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro. Although you can opt for the slightly cheaper Watch Fit 5 if you’re on a budget, the Pro model provides a much sturdier build and a brighter screen to boot.\n In fact, the larger 1.92-inch display (which isn’t that far off the 2-inch variant of the pricey Garmin Venu X1 ), can reach an absurdly bright 3000 nits, which is more than what you get from certain flagship wearables and even matches the peak of top-tier picks like the Apple Watch Ultra 3 . For a fitness tracker that costs just a fraction of the price of Apple’s outdoor watch, that’s incredible value for money.\n The new mini-workouts are fantastic for busy professionals or parents who only have a few minutes to spare throughout the day, and a helpful onscreen panda can show you how to perform the moves within the workout. You’ll also be prompted to do another mini-workout if the watch reckons that you’ve been stationary for too long, so it does a good job at making sure that you get in a bit of exercise where possible.\n All of this sits on top of the existing fitness tracking framework which is exemplary in its category. Not only are there tons of workouts to dive into, but Huawei’s sleep tracking software breaks down complex information into easy-to-understand advice. It’s a great watch overall, but bear in mind that the Apple Watch SE 3 costs less at £219 and boasts far more smart features, so it’s worth weighing up the type of features you’re most interested in before buying. \n Who should buy the Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro?\n Runners and gym goers who want a comprehensive fitness tracker with a great display.\n Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro: Further considerations\n The Fit 5 Pro is more expensive than the cheapest Apple Watch, which has a wider ecosystem of apps.\n Read our full \n Huawei Watch Fit 5 Pro review \n 5. Garmin Cirqa Smart Band – Best Affordable Garmin \n Pros\n Good value for money\n Comfortable, light design\n Dedicated workout button\n Plenty of subscription-free data\n Cons\n Relies heavily on the Garmin Connect app\n Missing some quality-of-life features\n Battery life shorter than stated\n Price When Reviewed: \n $199.99 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n Check \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n The Garmin Cirqa is a direct competitor to the Fitbit Air and the Whoop Strap 5.0, and just like those other screenless fitness trackers, the Cirqa provides a distraction-free means of keeping tabs on your health and fitness. What separates the Cirqa from the competition however is that it’s the one screenless wearable that really offers you the best value for money without a subscription attached.\n As much as we love the Fitbit Air, the only way to really make the most of that device is to sign up for the Google Health Coach. By comparison, although Garmin does offer a Connect+ subscription for AI-driven insights, it really isn’t necessary given the sheer amount of data that’s included with the base price. For example, after a run you can dive into everything from your stride length to your maximum speed and also your heart rate throughout the workout.\n What’s great is that, even though all that data is available for those who want to pore over the details, the Cirqa is one of Garmin’s more accessible watches for the fact that its sole push button can be used to start and stop the onboard workout tracking in an instant. You can then see what benefit that workout had on your overall fitness in the Garmin Connect app. It’s a great feature that gets you straight into the action, although we do wish that you could assign specific workouts to the button – by default it tracks a generalised ‘Timed Activity’ which omits certain data sets like the distance you’ve covered.\n Although the Garmin Connect app has more of a learning curve than the Google Health app, it has no shortage of ways to keep you motivated. The Garmin Coach feature can build a training plan for you to follow, which is handy if you’re just starting out and aren’t quite sure where to begin, while the Challenges tab has lots of digital badges that you can collect by achieving certain milestones. It’s because of features like these that Garmin has become so popular in the world of fitness, and the Cirqa offers up that ecosystem but at a much more affordable price.\n Who should buy the Garmin Cirqa?\n Those who want access to the Garmin ecosystem but at a cheaper price tag.\n Garmin Cirqa: Further considerations\n The Connect app has more of a learning curve than other fitness apps.\n Read our full \n Garmin Cirqa Smart Band review \n 6. Fitbit Charge 6 – Best Fitbit with a screen \n Pros\n Google Maps and Google Pay access\n Great fitness features\n Impressive colour display\n Easy navigation\n Cons\n Some Google Health exclusives\n No altimeter\n Hit-and-miss GPS\n Price When Reviewed: \n $159.95 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n 22% off original price $123.99 \n View Deal \n $124 \n View Deal \n $129.95 \n View Deal \n $129.95 \n View Deal \n $133.01 \n View Deal \n $159.95 \n View Deal \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n View more prices \n The Charge 6 takes what made the Charge 5 so good and makes it even better.\n Fitbit’s main addition this time around is access to Google Maps, Google Pay and YouTube Music (although no other music streaming services are available), making it closer to a smartwatch than ever before.\n Even with these changes, its primary focus is still fitness tracking with support for more than 40 workouts (some automatically) and your sleep. Data collected is extensive too, with the likes of steps, distance, calories burned, hourly activity and heart rate all monitored.\n The colour display is very impressive, and the device is easy to navigate using a combination or touch and the new side button. It’s also great to see built-in GPS, though it is unreliable at times.\n There’s still no altimeter, so you can’t measure floors climbed, while many advanced features are reserved for Google Health Premium subscribers. However, even if you don’t pay, the Charge 6 still delivers a very good all-round fitness tracking experience.\n Who should buy the Fitbit Charge 6?\n Anyone who appreciates an intuitive fitness tracking software that’s easy to follow.\n Fitbit Charge 6: Further Consideration\n Things can get expensive when you factor in the cost of Google Health Premium for all available features. \n Read our full \n Fitbit Charge 6 review \n 7. Amazfit Bip 6 – Best Screen \n Pros\n Excellent screen\n Plenty of features\n Long battery life\n Very cheap\n Cons\n App is a bit busy\n An odd storage bug\n AI assistant a bit disjointed\n Price When Reviewed: \n $79.99 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n 1% off original price $79 \n View Deal \n $79.99 \n View Deal \n $79.99 \n View Deal \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n Ever since its first iteration, the Amazfit Bip has punched above its weight in terms of features and performance. For the Bip 6, manufacturer Zepp has certainly gone big with the upgrades.\n For starters, it’s swapped TFT for a big and bright 1.97-inch AMOLED display and it instantly makes this budget smartwatch feel more premium.\n Features-wise, you’re getting the ability to make calls over Bluetooth, load on transport cards to make it easier to travel and have access to the Zepp Flow AI Assistant that’s worked well in some, but not all scenarios.\n In the fitness department, it has built-in GPS to track outdoor runs and is even capable of recording Hyrox workouts. It can track heart rate and blood oxygen levels, and even serve up useful scores to tell you if you’re ready to tackle a more intense workout. There’s also room for free offline maps for adventurers, which is a rare feature to find on a wearable this cheap. \n Topped off with a battery life of up to 14-days (or around 6 days in heavier use), and there are few better fitness trackers at this price that can compete with what the Amazfit Bip packs and delivers.\n Who should buy the Amazfit Bip 6?\n Those who value having a great screen above all other features.\n Amazfit Bip 6: Further Consideration\n Fitbits are a better option for a more intuitive fitness tracking experience.\n Read our full\n Amazfit Bip 6 review \n 8. Withings ScanWatch Light – Best Hybrid \n Pros\n Stunning design\n Clever display\n Impressive battery life\n Lightweight yet durable\n Cons\n Hit-and-miss step and heart rate tracking\n Not good for notifications\n No built-in GPS\n Price When Reviewed: \n $249.95 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n $249.95 \n View Deal \n Withings \n $249.95 \n View Deal \n $249.99 \n View Deal \n First Class Watches \n $330 \n View Deal \n $369.95 \n View Deal \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n View more prices \n Withings’ ScanWatch 2 might be the most complete hybrid watch you can buy, but the Light offers everything most people are looking for at a more affordable price.\n That includes a gorgeous premium design, which is relatively lightweight but still impressively durable. Within it you’ll find a tiny 0.63-inch monochrome display that is easy to control using the rotating physical crown.\n There’s enough room to display all key information except phone notifications, which often get annoyingly cut off. The ScanWatch Light can track over 40 workouts (some automatically), although data is generally limited to heart rate (including zones), steps, distance and pace.\n Sleep tracking is good and battery life is truly excellent. You’ll struggle to get the 30-day stint that Withings claims you can, but around 1-2 weeks is well within reach.\n If you can live without 24/7 temperature tracking, ECGs, irregular heart rhythm notifications and blood oxygen monitoring, the ScanWatch Light is the Withings watch to get.\n Who should buy the Withings ScanWatch Light?\n Anyone who wants to indulge in fitness tracking but with the cleaner look of an analogue dial.\n Withings ScanWatch Light: Further considerations\n The monochrome display is too small to read notifications comfortably.\n Read our full \n Withings ScanWatch Light review \n 9. Fitbit Inspire 3 – Best Cheap Fitbit \n Pros\n Always-on colour screen\n Stylish & comfortable\n Comprehensive tracking\n Good battery life & charging\n Cons\n Tiny screen\n Google Health Premium needed for some features\n Short charging wire\n No GPS\n Price When Reviewed: \n $99.95 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n 30% off original price $69.95 \n View Deal \n $69.95 \n View Deal \n $69.95 \n View Deal \n $69.95 \n View Deal \n $84.95 \n View Deal \n $89.95 \n View Deal \n $99.95 \n View Deal \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n View more prices \n The Inspire 3 is the cheapest model in the current Fitbit line-up, making it an excellent entry point into fitness tracking.\n It’s still more expensive than its rivals, but Fitbit’s software experience and ecosystem are both excellent, so for some it may be worth paying a little extra – even if paying more for such a small display does sting a little.\n It helps that you get up to 10 days of battery life (though a little less if you use the always-on display feature), as there’s 24/7 heart rate monitoring now bolstered by SpO2 data, as well as sleep tracking. Just bear in mind that there’s no built-in GPS support, and as with all Fitbits, you’ll need to subscribe to the paid Google Health Premium plan to get every bit of data out of it.\n Who should buy the Fitbit Inspire 3?\n Anyone who wants to get into the Fitbit ecosystem without spending too much money.\n Fitbit Inspire 3: Further considerations\n You can get more features from similarly priced competitors, not to mention larger screens.\n Read our full\n Fitbit Inspire 3 review \n 10. Garmin Lily 2 – Most Stylish \n Pros\n Lightweight and elegant \n Range of features\n Decent battery life\n Body Battery and stress tracking are excellent\n Cons\n No GPS\n Sleep tracking could be better\n Women’s health needs improvement\n Price When Reviewed: \n From $249.99 | Model reviewed $299.99 \n Best Prices Today: \n Retailer \n Price \n 3% off original price $240 \n View Deal \n $299.99 \n View Deal \n $299.99 \n View Deal \n $299.99 \n View Deal \n $299.99 \n View Deal \n Price comparison from over 24,000 stores worldwide \n View more prices \n The Garmin Lily 2 is one of the most stylish fitness trackers – especially if you want to wear one with smarter clothing.\n Activity tracking is accurate and the Body Battery and stress tracking features are a delight to use, giving a good insight into overall wellness.\n Sleep tracking could be better, as could women’s health tracking, and it doesn’t cater to hardcore athletes with a lack of built-in GPS and other features like recovery time, both of which are offered by the similar-priced Vivoactive range. Still, the Garmin Lily 2 excels for casual exercisers seeking a discreet and comfortable companion for their everyday wellness journey.\n For those with larger wrists or a strong focus on training metrics, the Vivoactive will be a better fit, but if you prioritise comfort, style and reliable fitness tracking in a compact package, the Garmin Lily 2 is a great option to consider.\n Who should buy the Garmin Lily 2?\n Anyone who’s fed up with boring-looking fitness trackers that don’t pair well with everyday fashion.\n Garmin Lily 2: Further considerations\n More robust smartwatches from Apple and Samsung are better for tracking women’s health.\n Read our full \n Garmin Lily 2 review \n Other fitness trackers tested\n The 10 devices featured above are our go-to recommendations right now in the world of fitness trackers, but they are far from the only options worth bearing in mind. With the sheer amount of fitness trackers we review, there’s no way that all of them could be included, but that hasn’t stopped some options from getting close.\n Huawei Watch GT Runner 2 – Though it uses the same Harmony OS platform found within the company’s smartwatches, this particular wearable is designed specifically around the practice of running, so if you want to get into better shape by doing just that then this is a great option to go for. GPS performance is outstanding, and it’s very lightweight too so it’s comfortable to wear over long periods of time.\n Xiaomi Smart Band 8 Pro – A more traditional fitness tracker that used to feature on this list, and while it is no longer sold by Xiaomi itself, the device can be picked up with quite a significant discount elsewhere. It’s ready for a dip in the pool with waterproofing up to 5ATM, and the large 1.74-inch AMOLED display makes it easy to scroll through menus and start workouts.\n Luna Band – Standing as a more affordable alternative to the Whoop Strap and the Garmin Cirqa, the Luna Band had a lot of potential but it just misses the mark on being a device that’s easy to recommend for everyone. To its credit, the accompanying software is intuitive to use, and the accuracy of its recorded data is similarly impressive, it’s just the design of the thing that doesn’t hold up to scrutiny as our testing unit picked up no shortage of scuffing.\n Curious to see more? Keep these other options on your radar:\n Huawei Watch D2 \n Huawei Watch Fit 4 \n Xiaomi Smart Band 9 \n Xiaomi Smart Band 9 Pro \n Fitbit Versa 4 \n Fitbit Sense 2 \n Samsung Galaxy Watch FE \n OnePlus Watch Lite \n Huawei Watch Fit 4 Pro \n Hannah Cowton-Barnes / Foundry\n Fitness tracker news that could affect your buying decision\n Although the Luna Band just missed the mark on adding another must-have device into the growing pool of screenless fitness trackers, it does seem as though the next major contender could be none other than Apple. After rumours swirled that Apple was working on its own Fitbit Air-style device , the most recent news is that the California-based company is now testing out prototypes for what the concept might look like.\n Because it’s so early in development, none of this is a guarantee that an Apple fitness tracker will actually see the light of day, and even if it does, we could be waiting until at least 2028 before it makes an appearance. Still, if everything comes together then this could be huge for iPhone users who want a screenless device that more seamlessly intertwines with the same apps and ecosystem that the Apple Watch currently enjoys.\n In the here and now, however, Google has finally launched the long-awaited successor the Fitbit Charge 6 , now known as the Fitbit Edge . Although it does bring some crucial fitness tracking upgrades including better integration with the Google Health Coach, the device is noticeably lacking in a few features that were commonplace on the Charge 6. We recommend holding out for our full review of the device before making a decision on whether it’s a worthy upgrade.\n Can I get a better fitness tracker deal?\n Thanks to our trusty deals widgets which sit alongside all of the entries on this list, you’ll be able to see the cheapest prices available immediately on this very page. Much like with smartwatches, fitness trackers do tend to be reduced during the year’s big sales events like Black Friday and Amazon Prime Day , but they’re not the only times to save.\n We’ve seen plenty of price drops for the latest fitness trackers at random points throughout the year, which is why it’s worth revisiting this page on a regular basis so that you can make use of any out-of-season discounts.\n How we test fitness trackers\n To gain a comprehensive understanding of their performance, we use each fitness tracker for at least a week in a range of scenarios, including outdoor runs and visits to the gym. We also compare fitness data with industry-standard equipment like chest-worn heart rate monitors to ascertain the accuracy of a given device.\n Read more about how we test wearables .\n Why you should trust Tech Advisor for smartwatch reviews and buying advice\n Ever since our founding, it has been Tech Advisor’s mission to bring you detailed yet easy-to-understand buying advice so you can make an informed decision with every device you buy. We never accept any form of payment for our reviews and the advice we provide is always unbiased and impartial.\n Who curated this article? \n This article was assembled by Tech Advisor’s Managing Editor, Chris Martin . As the man who oversees all content verticals on the site, few know their way around the tech industry quite like Chris, and that expertise has been used to verify that every device featured here is worthy of your shortlist.\n Martyn Casserly\n How to choose the best fitness tracker\n One of the few things you don’t have to worry about when it comes to fitness trackers is the price. By default, it’s rare to find a dedicated fitness tracker with a price that’s higher than £200/$200, so they’re already great buys for those who are tied to a budget. Instead, the key factor here will be the type of workout or area of health that you’re hoping to keep an eye on.\n For instance, if you’re an outdoor runner then GPS tracking is essential, but the accuracy of that tracking is made even better if you go with an option that has dual-band capabilities. Alternatively, for those who are most concerned with getting a good night’s rest and improving your overall sleep patterns then a wearable that actually breaks down sleep data into tangible advice is essential.\n It’s worth knowing ahead of time, just to clear up any potential confusion, but fitness trackers are not the wearables to go for when it comes to smart features. Sure, you’ll have a few basic elements like ‘find my phone’, calendar access and more, but unless you opt for a proper smartwatch, you won’t be accessing Nike Run Club or the Calm app via your wrist any time soon.\n 1. \n What is a fitness tracker?\n Fitness trackers help you measure and log a range of activities each day; from a simple step count to weightlifting sessions, swimming and more. Advanced models include sleep tracking, stress detection, and can even help monitor specific health problems.\n Some people will want a device that counts steps and doesn’t require frequent charging, whilst others might be in the market for a new marathon companion.\n Do you want to be able to log your routes using integrated GPS or is tethered GPS (pulling location data from your phone in real time) enough? Do you need constant heart rate monitoring? Does your tracker need to be water-resistant? Is integration with other fitness services (such as Strava) important? These are all things to consider before buying. \n 2. \n What’s the difference between fitness trackers and smartwatches?\n There’s no strict line here but broadly speaking, we define a smartwatch as a more advanced wearable with additional apps and functionality.\n You should expect a smartwatch to allow you to install and run apps – including some of the ones you’d find on your phone – as well as check notifications, reply to messages, and perhaps even take calls. Some smartwatches support eSIM for their own independent data connection.\n A fitness tracker is usually more simple, focusing on core health and fitness features. They tend to be smaller, cheaper and have a better battery life.\n Hybrid watches from companies such as Withings strike a balance between the two, but are still relatively expensive.\n 3. \n Should I just buy a Fitbit?\n Given that Fitbits are practically synonymous with fitness trackers at large, they’re almost always the first option considered when buying a wearable, but there are a few things to bear in mind. On the one hand, Fitbits are great for working adults who have a lot on their plates as the company’s software tracks your exercise over the course of a week, rather than a day. This means that if you have a busy few days, you can always make it up with a long gym session after work or at the weekend.\n Unfortunately, Google has stripped back a lot of Fitbit’s wider features in the last few years, and to get the full Fitbit experience, you’ll want to invest in Google Health Premium, which carries an annual cost of £79.99/$99.99. Google Health Premium is a good service in itself, but for folks who don’t want to spend too much and certainly don’t want to be hit with any added fees after the initial purchase, you’re better off buying a wearable from the likes of Xiaomi or Amazfit.\n Related content\n Smartwatch reviews \n Smartwatch news \n Smartwatch deals \n Best smartwatch \n Best budget smartwatch \n Best phones \n Best mid-range phones \n Best budget phones \n by \n Chris Martin \n Managing Editor, Tech Advisor \n Follow Chris Martin on X \n Follow Chris Martin on LinkedIn \n Tech Advisor Managing Editor Chris got his break as a reporter at infamous site The Inquirer and has been with us for more than 12 years. With a BA degree in Music Technology, audio is his specialism, but over the years he has reviewed all kinds of gadgets, from smartwatches to mesh Wi-Fi to coffee machines.\n More from this author\n news analysis \n Samsung’s foldable Galaxy Tab might be real \n Oct 30, 2026 4 mins \n news \n Motorola Signature 27 launch date revealed (finally) \n Oct 29, 2026 3 mins \n news \n Samsung’s teaser wasn’t a limited edition Galaxy Z Fold 8 after all \n Oct 28, 2026 3 mins \n news \n Motorola Signature 27 and Razr Flex prices leak ahead of launch \n Oct 28, 2026 3 mins \n opinion \n I’ve tried the Fitbit Edge and now I really want a Pixel Watch A \n Oct 28, 2026 4 mins \n best picks \n Best Smartwatch 2026: From Apple’s watchOS to Google’s Wear OS \n Oct 27, 2026 38 mins \n feature \n The first Oppo Find X10 Pro Max camera samples are here \n Oct 23, 2026 4 mins \n news \n ColorOS 17 is coming to these OnePlus phones and tablets – here’s when \n Oct 19, 2026 3 mins",
